#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""silkgate — host control CLI for the egress-proxied agent sandbox.

`run` is one-shot: proxy + guest born and torn down per command. Persistent
sessions instead share ONE background mitmdump (a pool of listener ports) across
many background guests — `up`/`exec`/`attach`/`down`, one guest per session, one
dedicated proxy port each. Host state lives under ~/.silkgate. See ../README.md.

    silkgate profiles              list the profiles in profiles/
    silkgate doctor                report every missing tool and its install command
    silkgate build --with X        build the image a profile list describes, into msb
    silkgate verify [--full]       Tier-1 containment check (test/verify_guest.sh)
    silkgate proxy --with X        run one ruleset on one port, audit on stdout
    silkgate run [-- CMD ...]      ephemeral session (up + exec + down), foreground
    silkgate up [--name N ...]     start a persistent session (bg guest + shared proxy)
    silkgate exec N [-t] -- CMD    run a command in a session's guest
    silkgate attach N              attach a TTY to a session, running its default command
    silkgate logs N [-f|--audit]   tail a session's guest output (or its proxy decisions)
    silkgate down N                stop + remove a session (+ the proxy if it was the last)
    silkgate ls                    list sessions + shared-proxy health
    silkgate secret set NAME       push EGRESS_SECRET_<NAME> to the shared proxy (never argv)
    silkgate secret ls             list secret names held by the proxy (names only)

A profile (profiles/<name>/) is one capability: setup.sh installs it at image build
time, rules.txt says what it may reach at run time. One `--with` list therefore
decides both the image — synthesized here, hash-tagged, cached in msb — and the
egress policy, so a guest can't hold a tool whose traffic nobody allowed.

Secrets stay in the environment: EGRESS_SECRET_<NAME>="<header>: <value>" on the
host feeds inject_auth=<name> rules; the guest never sees them.
"""
